course: dictionary
lesson: 11663
title: Assail: attack or overwhelm
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Assail is a formal word, so you will meet it more often in books and news than in chat. It means to attack someone violently, as in «The wolves assailed the flock in the night.»
explain: It has a second meaning too. To be assailed by doubts, worries, smells or questions is to have them come at you strongly from all sides. «She was assailed by questions from the reporters.» Nobody hit her. The questions simply came at her hard and fast.
explain: Look for clues. Words like attacked, army, storm or flooded with often sit close to assail. Try your meaning in the sentence: if «attacked» or «came at you strongly» makes sense, you have it.
fact: Assail is a verb. Its forms are assail, assails, assailed and assailing.
fact: «Assail» means to attack someone violently.
fact: «Assailed» can also mean that doubts, worries, smells or questions come at you strongly from all sides.
fact: «Assailed by a terrible smell» means the smell hit you hard and all at once.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Mole is assailed by fear in the Wild Wood.
exercise: «The army began to assail the castle at dawn.» What does assail mean here? | *To attack violently | To paint a picture of | To build a bridge over | To visit quietly
exercise: «Mole was assailed by a terrible smell from his old home.» What does assailed mean? | Was given a big present | Was fast asleep in bed | Ate a large dinner | *Had a strong smell hit him from all sides
exercise: Find and copy the word that means attacked in this sentence: «The wolves assailed the flock in the night.» | *assailed | night | wolves | flock
test: Choose the correct form: «Yesterday the crowd ___ the gates.» | assail | assails | assailing | *assailed
test: «She was assailed by doubts.» What does this suggest? | The doubts were asleep and gone | *Doubts kept coming at her strongly from all sides | She had answered every doubt and was sure | She was happy and had no worries
exam: Which sentence uses assail to mean attack violently? | The children walked slowly to school | The bird sang sweetly in the tree | The kettle boiled quietly on the stove | *The enemy ships assailed the harbour at night
